# LifePunch — Quality Bar (No Spaghetti)

This repo is a **reference** others will copy, and it will get much larger. Quality and
security beat speed. Maintain this across ALL of LifePunch (weapons, entities, website,
code) — not just the current task.

## Hack ≠ spaghetti (know the difference)
- A **hack / baseline** is a simple, pragmatic, *honest* shortcut or foundational stand-in.
  These are FINE — often necessary as we scale. Use them; just don't pretend an interim
  baseline is the polished endgame.
- **Spaghetti** is the real enemy: convoluted, unmaintainable code with a twisted, tangled
  control structure — complex interdependencies, no modular design, execution paths that
  jump unpredictably. A clean hack is contained and easy to swap; spaghetti is tangled and
  spreads. Avoid spaghetti.

## What counts as spaghetti (AVOID)
- Tangled interdependencies with no clear module boundary — you can't change one piece
  without breaking others.
- Workarounds stacked on workarounds (e.g. hidden-master + bonemerged passenger + invisible
  material all propping up one broken thing) — convoluted AND fragile.
- Dead/disabled components or orphaned assets left "just in case" that confuse the next reader.
- Wiring/control flow you'd be embarrassed to have copied because no one could follow it.

## The clean bar
- Prefer the engine's intended system in a **modular, swappable** shape (e.g. a real
  viewmodel rig with a `camera` bone + animgraph; config over forked systems; one class
  template reused as data). Prefer the fully-owned solution over depending on a third party
  shipping something.
- A baseline is acceptable when it is **simple, isolated, and has one obvious swap point.**

## Verify by stakes, not by which model wrote it
- Cost routing (cheaper models for routine work) is fine — but verification scales with **stakes**,
  not the model. Anything touching C# systems, the economy, permissions, or multi-file integration
  must be validated/tested or frontier-reviewed before it counts as done.
- A cheaper model producing plausible-but-subtly-wrong code is the risk; escalate to Opus the
  moment a task shows real complexity, and never auto-trust low-stakes-model output on high-stakes code.

## Be honest; track the temporary
1. Never present an interim baseline (or an unmaintainable mess) as the finished thing.
2. Offer the clean endgame so the user can choose baseline-now vs. clean-now.
3. If something is genuinely temporary, TRACK it in `lifepunchaddons/docs/TECH_DEBT.md`:
   what's temporary, why, the clean endgame, and the single swap point. A placeholder must
   never silently become permanent.

## Examples from this project
- ❌ vm_ak47 invisible-M4-master + bonemerged static AK + 3rd "passenger" renderer → empty
  hands: tangled, fragile, interdependent — that's **spaghetti**.
- ⚠️ vm_ak47 running the real M4 viewmodel as an honest, labeled **baseline** → fine *if*
  tracked in TECH_DEBT.md and orphan AK assets are removed. That's a hack, not spaghetti.
- ✅ Endgame: own a per-class viewmodel rig (camera bone + animgraph) and skin each weapon
  mesh to it — no dependency on Facepunch shipping source.
